#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161732 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "f3f6f5b1-bc25-586c-9d4d-dfeb64cfa077" :question/math-academy-id "e-8262" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $\\mathbf{x}$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\n1. $R(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 - 3x_2^2 + 5x_1x_2$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T(\\mathbf{x})=x_2^2 + 8x_1x_2$" :question/worked-solution "A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is negative-definite if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\lt 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n$R(x)|= 2^{2} - 3 \\cdot 7^{2} + 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 4 - 147 + 70; =-73 < 0$.\n- Quadratic form II *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$S(x)|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}; = 110 > 0$.\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n$T(x)|= 7^{2} + 8 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7; = 49 + 112; = 161 > 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "178e4bbc-718c-5b9e-843a-91914ef16f64" :question/math-academy-id "e-8263" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=9$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is indefinite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwe have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A$, we obtain\n$\\mid A - λI \\mid |= 0;\\begin{vmatrix}-4 - λ & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix}|= 0; (-4 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 25|= 0; λ^{2} + 8λ - 9|= 0; (λ - 1)(λ + 9)|= 0; λ|= 1,-9$\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $A$ has positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/id #uuid "fa658f82-1d0b-54a3-a172-4d59f03f629e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8264" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11}=-6$ and $\\det(A) \\lt 0$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\Delta_i$ for\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\ndenote the principal minors of an $n \\times n$ matrix $A$. Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$,\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $a_{11}=-6$. Expanding $\\det(A)$ along the top row, we get\n$det(A)|=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-5)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}; = (-6)(-2) + (5)(-7) + 12; =-11$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n$Δ_{1}|= a_{11} =-6 < 0; Δ_{2}|= \\mid - 6\\begin{vmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}- 2|= 37 > 0; Δ_{3}|= \\mid A|=-11 < 0$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{x}$, and the matrix $A$, are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86316dd3-aa16-5817-8ffb-5990c76a5d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160993} {:answer/id #uuid "c14f325e-6d20-5268-a3ff-d979946845ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160994} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d03e2e-c8b9-5761-8633-fa5679345b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160995} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc24c9a-a732-52dc-b36d-1ffcb58f62bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160996} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0ce6c1-d8ee-5ca4-8b76-47ccd5fd1ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the forms could be positive-definite" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc24c9a-a732-52dc-b36d-1ffcb58f62bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0914475a-5f56-5014-a2e3-f53a7b1654bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160992}] :question/id #uuid "19082f66-1046-53f5-8de5-47b2fc5eafb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94462" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n1. $R(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + 5x_{1}x_{2}$\n2. $S(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n3. $T(x) = x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3cc2de6-1735-5e4a-b4c1-486a0ddfba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161011} {:answer/id #uuid "bba45c46-a62f-5e18-810e-bc441ef2155c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161012} {:answer/id #uuid "df6ec1fc-e386-5dc6-9608-3c5f784b3e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161013} {:answer/id #uuid "5a646eb7-1c86-5c97-9778-74c6dee79c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161014} {:answer/id #uuid "797746b1-4ce4-5f0b-8573-7ad91b45b6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797746b1-4ce4-5f0b-8573-7ad91b45b6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161015} :answer-field/id #uuid "d00a818e-ea83-5439-9501-01a071b99919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161010}] :question/id #uuid "eb99fd8a-40c2-5ae9-b122-b1e03bf9e31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94465" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The eigenvalues of $A$ are $λ_{1} = 7$ and $λ_{2} = 10$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is positive-definite\n3. $A$ is indefinite"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b013ecb-d36f-5e38-a200-887a636ca980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161023} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d0b5e8-a540-5644-823e-4c1ceb795036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161024} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16d637-1ab3-51e5-8119-c7d1ccae8ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161025} {:answer/id #uuid "3a98e3aa-b99a-5ed4-8c78-1c0b5940519d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161026} {:answer/id #uuid "5b13330b-04f9-54d3-a37d-e28df3f21cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d0b5e8-a540-5644-823e-4c1ceb795036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161024} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb0048b6-efb6-5c75-b22f-963137e1cec8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161022}] :question/id #uuid "1a18cc33-e8b9-51a0-9ec6-b247bdd1e062" :question/math-academy-id "q-94467" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{11} =-1$ and $det(A) > 0$\n2. $x^{T}Ax$ is negative-definite\n3. $A$ is positive-definite"}]}
